1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:16,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to say something about if... My recollectedness, done properly, involves seeing yourself in feeling, rather than the feeling in you.

2
00:00:17,554 --> 00:00:18,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right, right, right.

3
00:00:19,095 --> 00:00:19,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

4
00:00:21,595 --> 00:00:25,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's the same principle as it always is.

5
00:00:26,397 --> 00:00:27,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Recollectedness of the body.

6
00:00:28,565 --> 00:00:42,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, when a person starts doing the, like, mindfulness of the body, it's sort of, you know, I'm attending to my body, and I'm mindful of the body, I'm bringing myself back to the body.

7
00:00:42,173 --> 00:00:51,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you put yourself first, your point of view first, your attentions first, and then it finds the body, because you were told you should do so.

8
00:00:52,316 --> 00:01:00,399
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the more you do it, if you do it correctly, if you start to understand that, actually, it's not about you attending to the body, but discerning that the body is there, so that you can attend it.

9
00:01:00,819 --> 00:01:03,100
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that knowledge of what's already there.

10
00:01:03,881 --> 00:01:16,526
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you already, in a way, start seeing as yourself within the container of the body, including your own thoughts, your own feelings, within the necessary basis of the body, not other way around, as in body exists only when you attend to it.

11
00:01:17,766 --> 00:01:19,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like... so...

12
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:25,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Being mindful of the body, you're kind of undermining that sense of self.

13
00:01:25,424 --> 00:01:29,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're fixing the perverted order of me first, body second.

14
00:01:31,588 --> 00:01:38,273
[SPEAKER_00]: But so... and by fixing the order, you're directly exactly undermining that... that... primacy that you have completely assumed.

15
00:01:38,453 --> 00:01:38,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Primacy?

16
00:01:38,693 --> 00:01:39,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Primacy, sorry.

17
00:01:39,134 --> 00:01:45,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,

18
00:01:46,672 --> 00:01:51,815
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm, I'm, I'm... my point of view and then this or that, depending on what I choose.

19
00:01:52,856 --> 00:01:57,058
[SPEAKER_00]: But the knowledge of, of the recollectedness is the knowledge.

20
00:01:57,438 --> 00:01:57,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

21
00:01:58,379 --> 00:02:02,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the necessary base of the body that endures regardless of what you attempt to do.

22
00:02:02,561 --> 00:02:03,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's discerning that.

23
00:02:03,702 --> 00:02:06,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you discern that, you see that you are within it.

24
00:02:06,303 --> 00:02:07,464
[SPEAKER_00]: As you cannot be outside of it.

25
00:02:07,564 --> 00:02:08,224
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

26
00:02:08,244 --> 00:02:14,248
[SPEAKER_00]: When you think outside of the body, that's within the base of the body enduring, that provides you with this experience.

27
00:02:15,961 --> 00:02:18,422
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the same comes down to the feeling as well.

28
00:02:18,702 --> 00:02:32,389
[SPEAKER_00]: See, when you're mindful of the feeling, it's immediately... because it sort of appears, one of its characteristics is kind of distant or more remote or less perceivable, so to speak, which means kind of you're assuming it's inside.

29
00:02:33,309 --> 00:02:33,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

30
00:02:33,669 --> 00:02:35,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Inside of this body or inside...

31
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:35,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Inside.

32
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:36,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inside of me.

33
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:38,591
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got a feeling in my head.

34
00:02:38,672 --> 00:02:41,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I am that thing that has feelings.

35
00:02:41,433 --> 00:02:41,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

36
00:02:43,553 --> 00:02:57,082
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you learn how to discern it as something that endures, regardless of whether you attend to it or not, you get to see that that feeling endures, and you are the one who goes left and right, up and down, in terms of your thinking or actions or choices.

37
00:02:57,482 --> 00:03:00,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start seeing yourself fully enclosed within the feeling.

38
00:03:02,325 --> 00:03:06,868
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another layer of that primacy being removed.

39
00:03:06,988 --> 00:03:07,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle.

40
00:03:07,669 --> 00:03:08,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle, yeah.

41
00:03:08,669 --> 00:03:10,851
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just taking it on a different phenomenon.

42
00:03:12,439 --> 00:03:21,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, again, the same, like, mind, the mood you're in, you would be regarding it as I am here, and the mood comes and goes.

43
00:03:22,248 --> 00:03:24,590
[SPEAKER_00]: But I remain here, and it's just I have different moods.

44
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:25,811
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other way around.

45
00:03:27,372 --> 00:03:29,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Mood is here, and you are second to it.

46
00:03:31,292 --> 00:03:36,875
[SPEAKER_00]: But because you always seem to be the same to you, to yourself, you assume that that's what stays unchanged.

47
00:03:37,555 --> 00:03:42,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But, doesn't matter how unchanged it stays, it's always dependent upon the present enduring mood.

48
00:03:42,617 --> 00:03:42,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

49
00:03:43,618 --> 00:03:46,279
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always dependent upon present enduring feeling.

50
00:03:46,299 --> 00:03:46,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

51
00:03:46,759 --> 00:03:56,824
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the Buddha asked, oh, if the present feeling, if the feeling is yours, when this this man said, yeah, feeling is mine, I'm like, basically, I'm the owner of my feelings, that's, that's where myself is.

52
00:03:57,464 --> 00:03:59,406
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, well, which feeling is it then?

53
00:03:59,686 --> 00:04:00,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there are three types.

54
00:04:00,787 --> 00:04:02,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't have them all three present.

55
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:06,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which means you have no say in their arising and enduring.

56
00:04:06,752 --> 00:04:08,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are regarding as if you are the first.

57
00:04:08,594 --> 00:04:09,334
[SPEAKER_00]: You are in charge.

58
00:04:10,195 --> 00:04:11,016
[SPEAKER_00]: You precede them.

59
00:04:12,037 --> 00:04:14,439
[SPEAKER_00]: You rule over them.

60
00:04:14,459 --> 00:04:15,200
[SPEAKER_00]: So, same with the moods.

61
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:16,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, moods come and go.

62
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:17,101
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they don't actually.

63
00:04:18,963 --> 00:04:22,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Moods come and go would be if you can step outside of yourself and see moods come and going.

64
00:04:23,723 --> 00:04:26,607
[SPEAKER_00]: You always have a mood enduring, which means mood is always there.

65
00:04:26,628 --> 00:04:27,509
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one mood.

66
00:04:27,709 --> 00:04:28,550
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one mood.

67
00:04:28,571 --> 00:04:29,532
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one feeling.

68
00:04:29,552 --> 00:04:30,353
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one body.

69
00:04:30,373 --> 00:04:31,215
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one body.

70
00:04:31,295 --> 00:04:32,116
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one image.

71
00:04:32,276 --> 00:04:33,418
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always one image, exactly.

72
00:04:35,161 --> 00:04:36,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Within which you are in.

73
00:04:38,222 --> 00:04:44,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you start seeing yourself second, it doesn't matter how much you feel like you're the one that does an unchanging phenomenon.

74
00:04:44,705 --> 00:04:45,245
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

75
00:04:45,826 --> 00:04:51,788
[SPEAKER_00]: If you still discern the mood that's presently enduring and then see yourself within it, not all the way around.

76
00:04:52,429 --> 00:04:55,270
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already... you're kind of aligning things rightly.

77
00:04:58,367 --> 00:05:03,892
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now the mood endures, you don't have to think about it, you can then do other things, and the mood is still there, and you don't have to keep attending to it either.

78
00:05:04,332 --> 00:05:07,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just, every now and then, look back, and is that mood still there?

79
00:05:07,475 --> 00:05:07,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

80
00:05:08,355 --> 00:05:09,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, what mood is still there?

81
00:05:09,777 --> 00:05:10,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mood is there.

82
00:05:11,378 --> 00:05:12,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Some mood has to be there.

83
00:05:12,919 --> 00:05:15,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Good, elated, depressed, something has to be there in a state of mind.

84
00:05:16,002 --> 00:05:18,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether the mood has changed, there's still mood.

85
00:05:18,224 --> 00:05:22,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and you're like, oh, you don't need to... oh, I must observe how the mood changes

86
00:05:27,422 --> 00:05:44,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness, we're calling it recollectedness, that seems to be a really good way of naming it, because it's about, basically, it's about recognizing something that's enduring.

87
00:05:44,136 --> 00:05:45,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

88
00:05:45,558 --> 00:05:47,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And then recollecting it every now and then.

89
00:05:47,639 --> 00:05:53,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in order, that's the thing, in order for you to, in order to recognize that it's enduring,

90
00:05:56,052 --> 00:06:00,035
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to... there has to be that kind of not staring at it.

91
00:06:00,335 --> 00:06:01,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly, yeah, yeah.

92
00:06:02,077 --> 00:06:08,081
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're staring at something... Well, if you're staring at something, you put yourself first, automatically.

93
00:06:08,101 --> 00:06:08,642
[SPEAKER_00]: You are, aren't you?

94
00:06:08,662 --> 00:06:09,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

95
00:06:09,863 --> 00:06:12,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the endurance of that thing, it's secondary.

96
00:06:12,725 --> 00:06:14,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's dependent on your attention.

97
00:06:14,046 --> 00:06:15,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, that's right, yeah.

98
00:06:16,579 --> 00:06:18,841
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why mindfulness, I kind of...

99
00:06:19,161 --> 00:06:24,505
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I use this because people can relate to it, but I don't like it because it's more associated with that attentiveness.

100
00:06:24,525 --> 00:06:24,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

101
00:06:25,125 --> 00:06:26,426
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm attending to this fully.

102
00:06:26,566 --> 00:06:27,827
[SPEAKER_00]: I am mindful of this.

103
00:06:28,268 --> 00:06:28,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

104
00:06:28,588 --> 00:06:31,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only know the thing is enduring if you're staring at it.

105
00:06:32,271 --> 00:06:32,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

106
00:06:32,771 --> 00:06:34,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, that's actually...

107
00:06:34,372 --> 00:06:35,793
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the opposite of recollectiveness.

108
00:06:35,813 --> 00:06:36,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

109
00:06:36,153 --> 00:06:37,214
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely the opposite.

110
00:06:37,254 --> 00:06:44,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, when you attend, you can only attend because the thing already endures that, before you attend it, and you know it, which is why you choose to attend it.

111
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:51,266
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you keep prioritizing your attention of the body, the body, the body, and not the knowledge that that body is there.

112
00:06:51,286 --> 00:06:52,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I ask you, do you still have arms?

113
00:06:52,827 --> 00:06:54,849
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't... Oh, wait, wait, let me check.

114
00:06:54,969 --> 00:06:56,030
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know the answer.

115
00:06:56,711 --> 00:07:09,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you want to learn how to discern that knowledge that you already know, of what's already there, that sort of precedes, at least structurally precedes, as in it comes first, within which you can attend and choose and so on.

116
00:07:10,988 --> 00:07:13,231
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you stop assuming outside of yourself.

117
00:07:13,271 --> 00:07:16,654
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you stop maintaining that, the whole notion of atavāda as well.

118
00:07:18,977 --> 00:07:22,701
[SPEAKER_00]: By putting the second as first, because it was meant to be first.

119
00:07:24,483 --> 00:07:27,447
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are putting that which is first as second, because you put yourself first.

120
00:07:28,668 --> 00:07:28,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

121
00:07:29,068 --> 00:07:32,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Or your point of view, or your attention, or your choice, and so on.

122
00:07:35,814 --> 00:07:45,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is a present feeling enduring, doesn't matter how distant, how small, how pleasant, how unpleasant it might feel, you regard that as this is the basis.

123
00:07:45,683 --> 00:07:48,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Any movement I make in regard to it is within that basis.

124
00:07:48,726 --> 00:07:51,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot step outside, cannot spill over, cannot become something else.

125
00:07:51,548 --> 00:07:52,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Something different to it.

126
00:07:52,209 --> 00:07:53,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Even your knowledge of it?

127
00:07:53,950 --> 00:07:54,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything.

128
00:07:54,431 --> 00:07:54,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything.

129
00:07:54,791 --> 00:07:55,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything.

130
00:07:55,111 --> 00:07:55,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

131
00:07:55,412 --> 00:07:55,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

132
00:07:58,074 --> 00:08:02,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing that occurred to me, that your knowledge of something

133
00:08:08,405 --> 00:08:10,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, one thinks of your knowledge of some...

134
00:08:10,966 --> 00:08:12,507
[SPEAKER_01]: I know this or I understand this.

135
00:08:12,947 --> 00:08:14,068
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something that I have.

136
00:08:14,088 --> 00:08:15,489
[SPEAKER_01]: I... it's something in me.

137
00:08:15,609 --> 00:08:15,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

138
00:08:16,169 --> 00:08:16,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

139
00:08:16,769 --> 00:08:19,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you are the one which is unchanging factor there.

140
00:08:20,651 --> 00:08:28,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, actually, properly understanding these phenomena is about letting them come first.

141
00:08:28,975 --> 00:08:33,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how they... letting them... letting them reveal themselves as what... as they are.

142
00:08:33,357 --> 00:08:34,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the knowledge is more in them

143
00:08:36,263 --> 00:08:38,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, in me, the knowledge is just that thing.

144
00:08:38,504 --> 00:08:39,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

145
00:08:39,144 --> 00:08:40,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Partially.

146
00:08:40,425 --> 00:08:42,886
[SPEAKER_00]: The only thing is, you need to know what to look for.

147
00:08:42,906 --> 00:08:47,168
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the instruction, external utterance of another needs to come first.

148
00:08:47,188 --> 00:08:47,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

149
00:08:48,629 --> 00:08:55,152
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, in rare cases, somebody can figure out themselves and that's when they become either Samasambuddha or Pacheco Buddha.

150
00:08:55,172 --> 00:08:55,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

151
00:08:55,833 --> 00:08:57,994
[SPEAKER_00]: But generally, people need pointers.

152
00:08:58,354 --> 00:09:01,856
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, yes, pointers not to like invent or create this knowledge.

153
00:09:01,896 --> 00:09:04,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Pointers to discern what's there.

154
00:09:04,097 --> 00:09:04,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

155
00:09:05,303 --> 00:09:05,863
[SPEAKER_00]: It is there.

156
00:09:06,283 --> 00:09:09,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you just know what to look for and then you get to see it, because it's there.

157
00:09:10,005 --> 00:09:11,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in you and not your creation.

158
00:09:11,585 --> 00:09:14,626
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the characteristics of the nature of things.

159
00:09:16,967 --> 00:09:23,149
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things we talk about in terms of how the body endures that beforehand and throughout.

160
00:09:23,249 --> 00:09:29,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you cannot, as I said, you cannot sort of disturb that order.

161
00:09:30,371 --> 00:09:31,372
[SPEAKER_00]: You can pretend you did.

162
00:09:32,454 --> 00:09:33,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can believe you did.

163
00:09:34,415 --> 00:09:37,677
[SPEAKER_00]: But your belief makes no difference to the factual order of things.

164
00:09:38,237 --> 00:09:38,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Body is first.

165
00:09:39,638 --> 00:09:40,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Body is your container.

166
00:09:41,199 --> 00:09:42,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Body is the full enclosure.

167
00:09:42,379 --> 00:09:43,380
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot step outside of it.

168
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:48,763
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything, even the notion of outside, is on the basis of that container of the body.

169
00:09:49,323 --> 00:09:51,384
[SPEAKER_00]: But how can you know the outside without the senses?

170
00:09:52,705 --> 00:09:59,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And senses are completely embodied, which means the notion of outside, real as such, is actually fully inside of the body, so to speak.

171
00:09:59,529 --> 00:10:01,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's inconceivable without it.

172
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:10,904
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like recollectiveness of the body in a nutshell, or just a principle of that recollectiveness, sati, in a nutshell.

173
00:10:12,525 --> 00:10:18,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's the feelings, the moods, that's the principle you want to see, that you are within it, not the other way around.

174
00:10:20,710 --> 00:10:24,052
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you automatically uproot the ownership.

175
00:10:25,353 --> 00:10:26,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Because usually people try to

176
00:10:28,097 --> 00:10:31,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand the non-self by saying to themselves that this is non-self.

177
00:10:32,499 --> 00:10:33,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, it's constantly changing.

178
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:34,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it cannot be mine.

179
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:39,242
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no thus in a direct insight.

180
00:10:39,922 --> 00:10:40,743
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no delay.

181
00:10:41,963 --> 00:10:45,685
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no, oh, this and then because of that, this is the result.

182
00:10:46,665 --> 00:10:48,646
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's with this, this is.

183
00:10:49,307 --> 00:10:52,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with the body first, my sense of self is not mine.

184
00:10:54,389 --> 00:10:56,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Without the body first, my sense of self is mine.

185
00:11:01,290 --> 00:11:08,918
[SPEAKER_00]: With ignorance, birth, aging, death, loss, whatever, craving, fighting and everything else that comes with it.

186
00:11:10,079 --> 00:11:12,862
[SPEAKER_00]: By putting yourself first, everything revolves around that.

187
00:11:12,902 --> 00:11:13,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

188
00:11:13,943 --> 00:11:14,323
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

189
00:11:14,804 --> 00:11:15,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

190
00:11:16,013 --> 00:11:20,917
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't decide, I'll put myself second, because you'll be doing that from the sense of self, of I.

191
00:11:21,397 --> 00:11:26,962
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can then discern what the Buddha said, which is the order as it is, regardless of your ignorance, your belief.

192
00:11:27,362 --> 00:11:34,328
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, even if I wish that this is first, and body second, even that wish is based on the body being first.

193
00:11:34,908 --> 00:11:43,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoroughly understanding that, all these wishes, beliefs, assumptions, ignorings cannot remain, because they can only remain because you're not looking at them.

194
00:11:43,475 --> 00:11:44,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

195
00:11:44,035 --> 00:11:46,196
[SPEAKER_00]: They're only there based on your assumption.

196
00:11:46,436 --> 00:11:46,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

197
00:11:47,036 --> 00:11:48,057
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't come first.

198
00:11:50,758 --> 00:11:51,738
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what it means.

199
00:11:53,319 --> 00:11:54,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness develops to the extent necessary.

200
00:11:56,380 --> 00:11:56,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

201
00:11:56,880 --> 00:12:00,501
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent necessary for being unable to assume the wrong order anymore.

202
00:12:02,122 --> 00:12:07,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Being unable to assume the wrong order means being unable to take up the ownership of your own senses, your own aggregates, your own sense of self.

203
00:12:30,266 --> 00:12:36,372
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, like, you can be practicing, like, regardless of the state of mind you're in.

204
00:12:37,913 --> 00:12:38,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, now I feel very clear.

205
00:12:39,434 --> 00:12:40,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I feel very muddled.

206
00:12:40,475 --> 00:12:40,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

207
00:12:40,996 --> 00:12:42,537
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, the body is there first.

208
00:12:42,917 --> 00:12:44,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at the expense of how you feel.

209
00:12:45,660 --> 00:12:47,101
[SPEAKER_00]: You just want to put it in the right order.

210
00:12:49,123 --> 00:12:51,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I couldn't feel anything if the body is not there alive.

211
00:12:51,605 --> 00:12:51,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

212
00:12:52,226 --> 00:12:54,568
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't be in this mood if the body is not there alive.

213
00:12:56,757 --> 00:13:02,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the feeling of the mood is always within the body?

214
00:13:02,901 --> 00:13:03,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

215
00:13:03,561 --> 00:13:10,365
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in itself, they can serve for the same principle of mindfulness, which means, if you're mindful of your feelings, basically, you're mindful of your body automatically.

216
00:13:10,405 --> 00:13:13,847
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're mindful of your mind, you're mindful of your body.

217
00:13:13,907 --> 00:13:17,910
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're mindful of the images and phenomena, you're mindful of your body.

218
00:13:20,061 --> 00:13:23,502
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, phenomena are much more subtle to discern this principle.

219
00:13:23,542 --> 00:13:25,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you see, thoughts are in me.

220
00:13:25,602 --> 00:13:26,362
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just always like this.

221
00:13:26,402 --> 00:13:27,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts are in my head.

222
00:13:27,283 --> 00:13:27,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts are there.

223
00:13:27,943 --> 00:13:28,503
[SPEAKER_00]: I have thoughts.

224
00:13:29,063 --> 00:13:34,484
[SPEAKER_00]: And it would be hard to see thoughts as the first and now within the enduring thought.

225
00:13:34,904 --> 00:13:37,905
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense of my point of view and everything else is within the enduring thought.

226
00:13:38,405 --> 00:13:45,727
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start doing that on the level of the body and maintain it, thoughts will have to be included, eventually.

227
00:13:47,793 --> 00:13:54,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so really it isn't so much the body or the feeling or the mood that's the important thing.

228
00:13:54,641 --> 00:13:58,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It is that structure, that kind of order.

229
00:13:59,487 --> 00:14:00,408
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

230
00:14:01,664 --> 00:14:11,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, oh, with this, this will be, or with this, like, when this has arisen, then this will come to be, and because of that, I cannot have it.

231
00:14:12,206 --> 00:14:17,687
[SPEAKER_00]: All that is delayed, all that is secondary, and all that's not a direct insight of the patīcampāra.

232
00:14:17,867 --> 00:14:18,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

233
00:14:22,048 --> 00:14:27,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just seeing the ground that makes any sense of self possible.

234
00:14:27,949 --> 00:14:28,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning the ground.

235
00:14:29,890 --> 00:14:30,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

236
00:14:30,311 --> 00:14:35,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, your non-discernment, your discernment, it's already... it cannot be elsewhere except within that ground.

237
00:14:51,887 --> 00:15:00,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness is not so much about you must, you know, focus on the body or, you know, keep staying with the body.

238
00:15:00,451 --> 00:15:04,673
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more... it's just recognizing anything, really, that's enduring.

239
00:15:05,294 --> 00:15:05,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

240
00:15:05,894 --> 00:15:06,874
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good starting point.

241
00:15:07,295 --> 00:15:10,496
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when it's completely the opposite direction of what you're attending at.

242
00:15:10,876 --> 00:15:11,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

243
00:15:12,537 --> 00:15:13,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that's enduring.

244
00:15:13,298 --> 00:15:17,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything you can attend to, it's already secondary to your senses.

245
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:17,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

246
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:17,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

247
00:15:18,631 --> 00:15:22,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone secondary to your point of view, to your choice, to your own attention.

248
00:15:25,273 --> 00:15:32,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the problem is in the senses, in the point of view, in your wrong attention, ayoniso, manasikāra.

249
00:15:33,255 --> 00:15:35,396
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot possibly see that, because you're focusing.

250
00:15:35,856 --> 00:15:42,478
[SPEAKER_00]: You're focusing on this, focusing on that, which means that which precedes that focusing, that which focusing is a result of, is completely invisible.

251
00:15:44,767 --> 00:15:48,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recollectiveness needs to start by discerning what is the basis to it all.

252
00:15:48,869 --> 00:15:50,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you focus or not doesn't matter.

253
00:15:50,949 --> 00:15:57,272
[SPEAKER_00]: This has to be an enduring basis, inseparable from, as the Buddha would say, inconceivable apart from.

254
00:16:01,694 --> 00:16:03,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And you already know that.

255
00:16:03,575 --> 00:16:07,256
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that we are so dependent on our own choices, attentions and focusing, i.e.

256
00:16:07,396 --> 00:16:11,818
[SPEAKER_00]: sense of control, that you even turn your dhamma meditation practice into that control.

257
00:16:13,674 --> 00:16:16,636
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you hope, somehow, results will kind of magically pop out of it.

258
00:16:17,657 --> 00:16:20,458
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, if I were to ask you, do you still have legs?

259
00:16:21,559 --> 00:16:23,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Are your arms still attached to your body?

260
00:16:23,620 --> 00:16:25,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes, immediately, immediately you'll say that.

261
00:16:26,322 --> 00:16:27,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you already know it.

262
00:16:27,383 --> 00:16:35,788
[SPEAKER_00]: If you genuinely don't know it, if you genuinely need to know it, you would be like, oh, what do we... You would have to then look and check and find out and gather information.

263
00:16:36,489 --> 00:16:40,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you can instantaneously answer means the knowledge is already there.

264
00:16:40,591 --> 00:16:41,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just not seeing it.

265
00:16:42,250 --> 00:16:44,733
[SPEAKER_00]: In the right light, so to speak, you're not seeing its relevance.

266
00:16:47,576 --> 00:17:02,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Relevance being, ah, that knowledge that I don't need to... that I cannot attend to, actually, is there, which is the reason I can attend and choose to attend these different parts of my body and so on, for whatever reasons I might do that.

267
00:17:03,925 --> 00:17:06,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, belief in your meditation technique or whatever.

268
00:17:06,627 --> 00:17:09,189
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, I need to have the body first.

269
00:17:09,830 --> 00:17:15,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's tapping into that, without needing to stare at it, look at it, make it into the object of your focus.

270
00:17:16,575 --> 00:17:17,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it won't be that.

271
00:17:18,136 --> 00:17:19,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't focus on it.

272
00:17:25,802 --> 00:17:27,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't focus on it, but you can know it.

273
00:17:28,664 --> 00:17:30,706
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like a practical rule of thumb.

274
00:17:30,786 --> 00:17:32,768
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're focusing on it, it's not it.

275
00:17:33,168 --> 00:17:33,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

276
00:17:34,272 --> 00:17:38,514
[SPEAKER_00]: If you know you're focusing, you're focused on whatever, whatever is right in front of me, I'm focused on that.

277
00:17:39,094 --> 00:17:41,295
[SPEAKER_00]: But what else do I know while I'm focused on that?

278
00:17:42,455 --> 00:17:44,376
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you start developing proper mindfulness.

279
00:17:45,616 --> 00:17:46,957
[SPEAKER_00]: By discerning what you already know.

280
00:17:47,697 --> 00:17:51,899
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it that makes it possible for me to... Yeah, what is the necessary basis for me?

281
00:17:52,039 --> 00:17:54,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm choosing to focus, I don't know, on that cup.

282
00:17:54,660 --> 00:17:57,281
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm focusing on it, I'm examining it, I'm looking at it, I'm thinking about it.

283
00:17:57,741 --> 00:18:03,503
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm even thinking about its significance and use and maybe I really like the cup, maybe I dislike it, whatever

284
00:18:05,204 --> 00:18:08,845
[SPEAKER_00]: All those movements of the mind, that's already all second and within.

285
00:18:09,685 --> 00:18:11,486
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say second, I don't mean it comes after.

286
00:18:11,866 --> 00:18:13,926
[SPEAKER_00]: I just mean it doesn't...

287
00:18:13,947 --> 00:18:18,088
[SPEAKER_00]: It requires or... Yeah, it's second in a structural manner.

288
00:18:18,108 --> 00:18:20,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a second floor is second to the first floor.

289
00:18:22,269 --> 00:18:23,829
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're both simultaneously there.

290
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:28,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be focusing on the cup for whatever reason.

291
00:18:28,891 --> 00:18:30,871
[SPEAKER_00]: You're focusing on the cup for whatever you choose to focus.

292
00:18:30,951 --> 00:18:33,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually things that, well,

293
00:18:34,007 --> 00:18:36,228
[SPEAKER_00]: They determine the currently enduring feeling as well.

294
00:18:37,089 --> 00:18:41,552
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what your attention would be, on the base of how you feel.

295
00:18:41,912 --> 00:18:44,674
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while that's enduring, yeah, you check.

296
00:18:44,874 --> 00:18:45,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the body still there?

297
00:18:46,595 --> 00:18:47,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Is something felt still there?

298
00:18:48,276 --> 00:18:49,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the mood still there?

299
00:18:50,297 --> 00:18:51,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, thoughts are still there.

300
00:18:52,319 --> 00:18:55,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what thoughts, what focusing phenomena.

301
00:18:56,121 --> 00:18:58,082
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is, it's there first.

302
00:18:58,162 --> 00:18:59,043
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already there.

303
00:18:59,103 --> 00:19:01,705
[SPEAKER_00]: It's enduring, which is why I cannot focus on this, this, this, this.

304
00:19:03,032 --> 00:19:11,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you also get to understand and discover the... like, your own intentions behind your focusing, your actions, your desire, your hopes, your efforts.

305
00:19:11,393 --> 00:19:12,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

306
00:19:12,154 --> 00:19:13,474
[SPEAKER_00]: My personal... Yeah.

307
00:19:13,574 --> 00:19:18,295
[SPEAKER_00]: ...thing that I can... Because they're always... that intention is always rooted in the enduring feeling.

308
00:19:18,315 --> 00:19:18,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

309
00:19:18,695 --> 00:19:19,315
[SPEAKER_00]: In how you feel.

310
00:19:19,335 --> 00:19:19,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

311
00:19:19,575 --> 00:19:23,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And that has to be there for... throughout of your endeavors.

312
00:19:23,256 --> 00:19:23,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

313
00:19:24,236 --> 00:19:28,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if that thing changes, your endeavor will stop, because motivation is gone.

314
00:19:28,356 --> 00:19:30,777
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be doing it anymore.

315
00:19:30,857 --> 00:19:31,217
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess.

316
00:19:35,634 --> 00:19:47,499
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess it's quite possible for people to think that a feeling is not necessary, but it's actually... it's just like... Yeah, because, again, they still see themselves at first.

317
00:19:48,119 --> 00:19:48,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

318
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:49,680
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, feeling is circumstantial.

319
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:50,020
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

320
00:19:50,040 --> 00:19:51,061
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can dismiss it.

321
00:19:51,081 --> 00:19:51,981
[SPEAKER_01]: This thing that...

322
00:19:52,641 --> 00:19:54,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, I'm assailed by feelings.

323
00:19:54,962 --> 00:19:56,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of the time, I'm not, really.

324
00:19:56,183 --> 00:20:00,945
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when the wrong order has been so perverted and, like, almost, like, like, like, solidified.

325
00:20:01,005 --> 00:20:01,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

326
00:20:01,345 --> 00:20:03,866
[SPEAKER_00]: In that... in that... in that wrong order.

327
00:20:03,926 --> 00:20:04,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

328
00:20:04,767 --> 00:20:05,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And now that...

329
00:20:06,465 --> 00:20:06,906
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

330
00:20:07,827 --> 00:20:09,209
[SPEAKER_00]: This is how it is.

331
00:20:09,289 --> 00:20:10,330
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need this, I don't need that.

332
00:20:10,771 --> 00:20:12,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you need to start overriding.

333
00:20:12,834 --> 00:20:16,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, all you need to ask yourself is, well, are you liable to suffering?

334
00:20:16,619 --> 00:20:21,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you remain truly, truly indifferent to whatever feeling comes your way?

335
00:20:22,767 --> 00:20:29,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only answer, the only way you can say that yes, is if you're an arahant, which means you would have undone the order, and then you would know what I'm talking about.

336
00:20:29,590 --> 00:20:33,232
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you say, oh no, I don't need, like, feelings come and go, I'm not, I'm not affected by them.

337
00:20:33,272 --> 00:20:44,077
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you believe you're not affected by them, now, because nothing bothers you now, but you're still affected by the pleasure, which means you will be affected by the bother, by the loss of pleasure, by not enough pleasure.

338
00:20:44,798 --> 00:20:47,699
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things, as the Buddha described, as the, as the base for suffering.

339
00:20:52,382 --> 00:20:57,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, okay, so the knowledge is there already, I'm just choosing to ignore it because it makes me feel unpleasant.

340
00:20:57,765 --> 00:21:01,567
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge that I am liable to feelings, which means feelings precede me.

341
00:21:02,448 --> 00:21:06,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings are more fundamental than me and my ownership of them.

342
00:21:07,571 --> 00:21:12,494
[SPEAKER_00]: And they come and go on their own in a sense of I depend on their coming and going, yet I have no control over it.

343
00:21:12,554 --> 00:21:15,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, oh, they come and go in order to dismiss them.

344
00:21:15,756 --> 00:21:15,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

345
00:21:15,976 --> 00:21:17,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people go, everything shall pass.

346
00:21:18,578 --> 00:21:19,298
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it won't.

347
00:21:20,020 --> 00:21:21,381
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a good way of putting it.

348
00:21:21,461 --> 00:21:23,282
[SPEAKER_01]: I depend on them, but I can't control them.

349
00:21:23,442 --> 00:21:23,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

350
00:21:23,642 --> 00:21:25,263
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter if they're coming and going.

351
00:21:25,283 --> 00:21:25,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

352
00:21:27,325 --> 00:21:31,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether they're coming, whether they're going, you're affected by that, by coming and going.

353
00:21:32,608 --> 00:21:38,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people say, oh, it's impermanent, it's impermanent, it will pass, they do it just to, like, just, oh, okay, it will end.

354
00:21:38,211 --> 00:21:38,752
[SPEAKER_01]: To manage it.

355
00:21:38,772 --> 00:21:38,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

356
00:21:39,052 --> 00:21:40,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to manage it, exactly.

357
00:21:40,333 --> 00:21:41,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But not, that cannot uproot it.

358
00:21:42,014 --> 00:21:42,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

359
00:21:42,925 --> 00:21:51,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're still assuming that... Well, yeah, you are liable to them, whether you believe in it or not, whether you believe else... otherwise, it's... that is the factual state of things.

360
00:21:52,068 --> 00:21:52,848
[SPEAKER_00]: They come first.

361
00:21:53,868 --> 00:22:11,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless... and the same, like, with all those similes in terms of spirituality and so on, it relates to this, like, oh, you climb the tree in the pursuit of pleasures and so on, to eat mangoes and whatnot, and, you know, that tree will have to come down, either somebody will cut it or what, and if you don't come down, you will be killed by falling from great height.

362
00:22:12,842 --> 00:22:13,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, same.

363
00:22:13,282 --> 00:22:17,103
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who goes into the domain of feelings, the body, through your ownership assumption.

364
00:22:17,484 --> 00:22:24,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, as the Buddha said, like borrowing, borrowing jewelry, borrowing gold, and then becoming dependent on that pleasure and admiration that you get from it.

365
00:22:25,346 --> 00:22:30,448
[SPEAKER_00]: But, deep down, you know you took it, you borrowed it, you did not create it.

366
00:22:30,468 --> 00:22:30,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

367
00:22:30,848 --> 00:22:33,189
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not... it's, it's, it's...

368
00:22:33,209 --> 00:22:35,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Think that the owner could come at that moment.

369
00:22:35,549 --> 00:22:36,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you know that the owner will come.

370
00:22:36,970 --> 00:22:37,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

371
00:22:37,823 --> 00:22:41,164
[SPEAKER_01]: You just choose to believe that, like, you'll deal with it.

372
00:22:41,264 --> 00:22:42,264
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be affected by it.

373
00:22:42,284 --> 00:22:43,504
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll deal with that when that happens.

374
00:22:43,885 --> 00:22:49,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And usually, if you maintain with that, in that, keep that view, it'll be too late to do anything about it.

375
00:22:49,966 --> 00:22:50,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yeah.

376
00:22:51,446 --> 00:22:54,087
[SPEAKER_00]: The longer you keep that view, the harder it will be to undo it.

377
00:22:54,127 --> 00:22:56,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you kind of grow accustomed to it.

378
00:22:56,548 --> 00:22:56,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

379
00:22:57,408 --> 00:22:58,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, oh, so where's the problem now?

380
00:22:58,988 --> 00:23:02,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So I start focusing, I'll focus on my body and maybe, like, you know, become familiar with it.

381
00:23:03,469 --> 00:23:04,749
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the result of the problem.

382
00:23:05,630 --> 00:23:06,170
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is

383
00:23:08,425 --> 00:23:11,787
[SPEAKER_00]: To what happens to the body, you cannot remain emotionally indifferent.

384
00:23:12,867 --> 00:23:15,669
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens to feelings, you cannot remain emotionally indifferent.

385
00:23:16,249 --> 00:23:18,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Moods, you cannot remain emotionally indifferent.

386
00:23:18,870 --> 00:23:19,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

387
00:23:19,791 --> 00:23:27,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are still, again, by assuming that you come first, and then second, you make yourself liable to them.

388
00:23:27,935 --> 00:23:29,756
[SPEAKER_00]: By seeing it correctly, you

389
00:23:30,220 --> 00:23:31,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, you pull yourself out of it.

390
00:23:31,741 --> 00:23:36,145
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not anymore engaging with on... and that's why you abandon the Amāra's domain.

391
00:23:36,546 --> 00:23:37,747
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't chase Amāra away.

392
00:23:38,187 --> 00:23:39,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one in his property.

393
00:23:40,449 --> 00:23:46,975
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, what is... the Buddha said, what is the property of Amāra that you take, because of which, you know, everything can ascend you, Amāra can control you.

394
00:23:46,995 --> 00:23:50,798
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the eye, the sights, the nose, the smells, all the senses.

395
00:23:51,419 --> 00:23:53,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And he goes, okay, so it's not mine.

396
00:23:53,321 --> 00:23:54,422
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that can be Amāra's.

397
00:23:55,573 --> 00:23:57,354
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not about, oh, this is not mine, this is not mine.

398
00:23:57,414 --> 00:24:05,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about seeing it coming first, enduring first, being given to you for use, and then maintaining that context, maintaining that knowledge.

399
00:24:06,601 --> 00:24:08,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I can ask you now, can you focus on your feet?

400
00:24:11,204 --> 00:24:12,005
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you?

401
00:24:12,085 --> 00:24:14,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel hot or cold in your feet?

402
00:24:14,446 --> 00:24:14,947
[SPEAKER_00]: A bit cold.

403
00:24:15,287 --> 00:24:15,948
[SPEAKER_00]: A bit cold.

404
00:24:15,968 --> 00:24:18,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you focus on a particular aspect of your body.

405
00:24:18,669 --> 00:24:19,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

406
00:24:20,551 --> 00:24:23,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Would it be possible for you to focus on your feet, to tell me

407
00:24:24,082 --> 00:24:25,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever perception is down there.

408
00:24:25,664 --> 00:24:25,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

409
00:24:26,264 --> 00:24:27,366
[SPEAKER_00]: If the body is not there.

410
00:24:27,386 --> 00:24:29,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course not.

411
00:24:29,608 --> 00:24:32,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you completely ignore that which you already know.

412
00:24:32,211 --> 00:24:32,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

413
00:24:32,671 --> 00:24:33,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And then start to just like...

414
00:24:33,832 --> 00:24:34,473
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just focus.

415
00:24:34,513 --> 00:24:39,358
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just look closely and closer and closer and somehow see the magical reality behind it.

416
00:24:40,740 --> 00:24:45,505
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you find... if you find yourself, for example, can't help but focusing on this or focusing on that,

417
00:24:46,370 --> 00:24:47,211
[SPEAKER_00]: You think, I must stop this.

418
00:24:47,251 --> 00:24:47,471
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

419
00:24:47,751 --> 00:24:51,193
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to see that the body is a necessary base, enduring, while you're focusing.

420
00:24:51,353 --> 00:24:51,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

421
00:24:51,633 --> 00:24:53,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And then your focusing becomes redundant.

422
00:24:53,354 --> 00:24:55,496
[SPEAKER_00]: You will focus only when there is a purpose to focus.

423
00:24:55,816 --> 00:24:55,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

424
00:24:56,276 --> 00:24:59,558
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't focus for some projected idealistic purpose of enlightenment.

425
00:24:59,578 --> 00:25:00,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Some practice.

426
00:25:00,158 --> 00:25:00,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

427
00:25:00,298 --> 00:25:00,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

428
00:25:02,140 --> 00:25:15,908
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the possibility of... the fact that I'm focusing on my left cold foot right now, is just one of the options that have been presented by this body.

429
00:25:16,653 --> 00:25:17,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

430
00:25:17,977 --> 00:25:21,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And the irony of it is, the more you

431
00:25:22,733 --> 00:25:26,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Prioritize focusing in order for you to discover your body.

432
00:25:26,254 --> 00:25:26,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

433
00:25:26,634 --> 00:25:28,174
[SPEAKER_00]: The further away from the body you are.

434
00:25:28,194 --> 00:25:29,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you are, yeah.

435
00:25:29,275 --> 00:25:32,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your body is that knowledge that precedes the focusing.

436
00:25:32,395 --> 00:25:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, your notion.

437
00:25:33,115 --> 00:25:45,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are so unaware of it, you have no means of discerning it, that the only way you can get a sense of body is with these body sweeping techniques and focusing of knees and shoulders and belly and top of my head and tongue.

438
00:25:45,878 --> 00:25:49,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, okay, keep doing, keep moving, keep moving and then you get a sense of body.

439
00:25:49,979 --> 00:25:52,520
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already like a secondary second.

440
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:53,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

441
00:25:53,660 --> 00:26:01,021
[SPEAKER_00]: To the sense of body, that's a necessary basis for you to even begin that focusing, let alone recreate another image of the body from your focusing.

442
00:26:01,262 --> 00:26:03,202
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it's kind of... it's reductionist, isn't it?

443
00:26:03,242 --> 00:26:04,862
[SPEAKER_00]: I just realized as you said that.

444
00:26:05,022 --> 00:26:07,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's reductionist in order to rebuild it.

445
00:26:07,143 --> 00:26:07,583
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

446
00:26:07,603 --> 00:26:08,003
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

447
00:26:08,023 --> 00:26:14,984
[SPEAKER_01]: You... you focus on this, then you focus on that, and you focus on that, so that you can add it all together to create this nice little... And... and... what?

448
00:26:15,664 --> 00:26:18,445
[SPEAKER_00]: And what remains first throughout that?

449
00:26:18,565 --> 00:26:20,705
[SPEAKER_00]: The body that makes all that possible in the first place.

450
00:26:21,125 --> 00:26:21,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Factually.

451
00:26:21,886 --> 00:26:24,968
[SPEAKER_00]: But for the person who does that, it's their sense of self remains first.

452
00:26:25,169 --> 00:26:25,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, sorry.

453
00:26:26,229 --> 00:26:29,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when they rediscover their body through their cessation watching.

454
00:26:29,172 --> 00:26:29,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

455
00:26:29,933 --> 00:26:30,373
[SPEAKER_00]: They're creating.

456
00:26:30,413 --> 00:26:33,015
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still, oh, I'm gonna create it.

457
00:26:33,115 --> 00:26:34,837
[SPEAKER_00]: This has resulted from my effort.

458
00:26:34,877 --> 00:26:35,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

459
00:26:35,537 --> 00:26:36,698
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I am the first.

460
00:26:36,738 --> 00:26:38,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

461
00:26:39,321 --> 00:26:42,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, it comes down to sense of control.

462
00:26:43,524 --> 00:26:47,908
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, it comes down to pursuit and fulfillment, which is the principle of sensuality as well.

463
00:26:51,680 --> 00:26:51,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

464
00:26:52,280 --> 00:27:02,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you end up doing that, you're observing senses, on the basis of the senses, pursuing it, getting what you want, building what you want, seeing what you want, you're just meditating with your own centrality.

465
00:27:02,703 --> 00:27:02,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

466
00:27:03,564 --> 00:27:04,784
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha so many times said.

467
00:27:05,664 --> 00:27:07,165
[SPEAKER_00]: And he literally, like, focusing.

468
00:27:08,345 --> 00:27:13,126
[SPEAKER_00]: You know how people say, oh, you can, like, like, the soldier is focused on his aim, it's mindful.

469
00:27:13,367 --> 00:27:13,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

470
00:27:14,407 --> 00:27:14,887
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh,

471
00:27:16,601 --> 00:27:19,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Often, you hear similar kind of comparisons.

472
00:27:20,884 --> 00:27:21,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, exactly.

473
00:27:21,685 --> 00:27:28,270
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you find a certain bit where it says, yeah, yeah, it's like a cat focused on a hole where a mouse is there.

474
00:27:28,310 --> 00:27:32,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Or an owl focused on a mouse, a cat on a fish, a crane on a fish.

475
00:27:32,133 --> 00:27:34,355
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember anymore which animal was hunting which one.

476
00:27:34,896 --> 00:27:39,019
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize, ah, so it was the hunter's focusing type.

477
00:27:39,339 --> 00:27:40,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Like full-on focus.

478
00:27:40,360 --> 00:27:42,141
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing else exists for me.

479
00:27:42,161 --> 00:27:44,023
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm 100% focused and my aim

480
00:27:44,958 --> 00:27:48,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said that is meditating with sensuality.

481
00:27:48,519 --> 00:27:48,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

482
00:27:49,219 --> 00:27:52,380
[SPEAKER_00]: But because of that, you over-meditate, mis-pre.

483
00:27:52,560 --> 00:27:52,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

484
00:27:53,240 --> 00:27:57,781
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot meditate because you don't see the background, you don't see the right context for which you need knowledge.

485
00:27:58,862 --> 00:28:04,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your practice is practice of focusing, sensation watching or aiming or, you know, narrowing,

486
00:28:04,948 --> 00:28:10,733
[SPEAKER_00]: You are like a jackal, like a cat, like an owl, like an animal trying to catch its prey.

487
00:28:10,773 --> 00:28:13,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And people think that's kind of lofty.

488
00:28:14,176 --> 00:28:15,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's like one-mindedness.

489
00:28:15,878 --> 00:28:17,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha mocked it.

490
00:28:17,479 --> 00:28:18,840
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha mocked it.

491
00:28:19,181 --> 00:28:22,163
[SPEAKER_00]: They call them, people who meditate like that, call themselves meditators.

492
00:28:22,584 --> 00:28:26,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here they are, shoulders and head drooping and whatever else.

493
00:28:26,827 --> 00:28:29,450
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's all they do is meditate with their own centrality.

494
00:28:29,950 --> 00:28:38,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, you might not be pursuing the sense object in itself, in terms of the common sense objects of pleasure, but you're still pursuing the objects of your focusing.

495
00:28:38,333 --> 00:28:38,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

496
00:28:38,834 --> 00:28:40,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means they're still objects of your senses.

497
00:28:40,954 --> 00:28:41,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I guess that...

498
00:28:41,835 --> 00:28:43,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still objects of your sensuality.

499
00:28:43,936 --> 00:28:47,137
[SPEAKER_01]: But that move away from the grosser...

500
00:28:48,678 --> 00:28:53,022
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of sensuality feels like, oh, this is healthy, this is good.

501
00:28:53,042 --> 00:28:53,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

502
00:28:53,723 --> 00:28:57,526
[SPEAKER_00]: But you only move away from it, like, circumstantially.

503
00:28:57,586 --> 00:28:57,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

504
00:28:58,047 --> 00:28:59,988
[SPEAKER_00]: In the nature, you're still within the same framework.

505
00:29:00,008 --> 00:29:00,889
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said.

506
00:29:00,929 --> 00:29:04,693
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how much you do that type of meditation, you're still within the domain of sensuality.

507
00:29:05,124 --> 00:29:09,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And the danger is, of course... Well, the danger is you can't sustain that.

508
00:29:09,245 --> 00:29:09,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

509
00:29:10,085 --> 00:29:16,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You can focus only at certain periods of time and, yeah, you can push yourself eight hours a day, do a three-month retreat in whatever.

510
00:29:17,307 --> 00:29:18,967
[SPEAKER_00]: But you develop views on top of it as well.

511
00:29:19,047 --> 00:29:21,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you cannot remain indifferent.

512
00:29:21,188 --> 00:29:22,288
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot remain indifferent.

513
00:29:22,868 --> 00:29:33,651
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that you are drilling down in the direction of central focusing, so far away from the context that's necessary for even you beginning to do it, means the further you go, the less you'll be able to see it.

514
00:29:34,051 --> 00:29:34,591
[SPEAKER_01]: And so...

515
00:29:35,631 --> 00:29:40,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Your understanding of what the body is, is... Oh, it's going to be a screen skew.

516
00:29:42,076 --> 00:29:47,080
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, like, yeah, exactly, far and far removed from what actually is there.

517
00:29:47,361 --> 00:29:52,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could still technically know it, if you were to decide, oh, wait a minute, I already know this.

518
00:29:52,465 --> 00:29:53,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have a body?

519
00:29:53,145 --> 00:29:53,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

520
00:29:53,806 --> 00:29:54,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

521
00:29:54,446 --> 00:29:56,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I develop that knowledge without needing to focus on it.

522
00:29:57,468 --> 00:30:01,952
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's going to be harder and harder to do, because each time you choose to double down on your focusing technique,

523
00:30:02,992 --> 00:30:13,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Aim at a prey, or whatever goal you want, each time you end up investing yourself more, which means you're going to have more views on account of it, more significance to it, and it will be harder to undo it.

524
00:30:16,363 --> 00:30:20,286
[SPEAKER_00]: And same with the object of my meditation.

525
00:30:20,306 --> 00:30:24,429
[SPEAKER_00]: When people ask for the object to focus on, it's literally, tell me what to hunt.

526
00:30:24,529 --> 00:30:25,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

527
00:30:25,830 --> 00:30:31,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell me what to stare at centrally, with the senses assumed first.

528
00:30:33,283 --> 00:30:34,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Yuri, come on, man.

529
00:30:34,203 --> 00:30:34,623
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you go?

530
00:30:35,263 --> 00:30:35,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Go back.

531
00:30:37,584 --> 00:30:40,004
[SPEAKER_00]: The... Tell me what to... what to khandhā.

532
00:30:40,404 --> 00:30:43,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell me what to... what to... what to watch and observe.

533
00:30:43,425 --> 00:30:44,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, not tell me what to understand.

534
00:30:45,245 --> 00:30:45,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

535
00:30:48,086 --> 00:30:52,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the object of meditation should be that which you cannot make a focusing object.

536
00:30:52,606 --> 00:30:53,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's what I was thinking, yeah.

537
00:30:53,767 --> 00:30:54,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need...

538
00:30:54,267 --> 00:30:57,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's about understanding phenomena.

539
00:30:57,667 --> 00:31:00,888
[SPEAKER_01]: The understanding... We're talking about body.

540
00:31:01,948 --> 00:31:02,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh...

541
00:31:03,257 --> 00:31:04,517
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you... do you feel anything now?

542
00:31:04,758 --> 00:31:05,198
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you feel?

543
00:31:05,338 --> 00:31:06,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant, unpleasant, neutral?

544
00:31:07,299 --> 00:31:16,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So, did you need to stop sitting, resting your arms, thinking what you're about to think, in order to find out that answer?

545
00:31:16,402 --> 00:31:17,162
[SPEAKER_00]: That was already there.

546
00:31:17,442 --> 00:31:18,403
[SPEAKER_00]: You answered immediately.

547
00:31:18,543 --> 00:31:20,604
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as I finished a sentence, you asked me what you felt.

548
00:31:20,884 --> 00:31:21,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

549
00:31:21,204 --> 00:31:21,884
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already there.

550
00:31:22,144 --> 00:31:22,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

551
00:31:23,084 --> 00:31:28,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, learn how to then see what's already there, without needing to stop doing everything else and attend to it and find it.

552
00:31:28,687 --> 00:31:29,687
[SPEAKER_00]: And in doing that,

553
00:31:38,202 --> 00:31:49,069
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to say, you're sort of... it's coming... becoming familiar with something that you... you can't know by looking at it.

554
00:31:50,209 --> 00:31:50,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

555
00:31:50,549 --> 00:31:51,830
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't observe your feeling.

556
00:31:52,010 --> 00:31:52,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

557
00:31:53,751 --> 00:31:54,552
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only feel it.

558
00:32:00,375 --> 00:32:06,279
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because that comes first, and I'm fully enclosed within it,

559
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:16,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I, I, it's like, I can't... You can't say what side of it.

560
00:32:16,383 --> 00:32:17,583
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't... You can't relate to it.

561
00:32:17,663 --> 00:32:18,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Relate to it.

562
00:32:18,444 --> 00:32:19,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't... No, exactly.

563
00:32:19,744 --> 00:32:22,866
[SPEAKER_01]: I just have to allow that thing to reveal itself.

564
00:32:23,126 --> 00:32:23,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

565
00:32:23,606 --> 00:32:28,148
[SPEAKER_00]: And you, even when you forget about it, your forgetting of it is within it.

566
00:32:28,509 --> 00:32:28,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

567
00:32:28,689 --> 00:32:29,569
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where you can remember it.

568
00:32:29,589 --> 00:32:29,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

569
00:32:30,409 --> 00:32:31,410
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where you don't need to...

570
00:32:31,490 --> 00:32:32,190
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm always...

571
00:32:32,250 --> 00:32:33,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I must always have it present in my mind.

572
00:32:33,871 --> 00:32:34,591
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

573
00:32:35,212 --> 00:32:35,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

574
00:32:35,962 --> 00:32:38,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your mind, you are a result of it.

575
00:32:39,284 --> 00:32:39,925
[SPEAKER_00]: You're within it.

576
00:32:39,965 --> 00:32:42,106
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't choose to hold this presently.

577
00:32:42,827 --> 00:32:46,689
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's going to be present for as long as it is present by itself, in itself.

578
00:32:46,709 --> 00:32:48,290
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what...

579
00:32:48,611 --> 00:32:58,578
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to say, I know this or I understand this, almost the old way would be, I am doing...

580
00:32:59,598 --> 00:33:00,159
[SPEAKER_01]: I come first.

581
00:33:00,179 --> 00:33:00,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

582
00:33:00,819 --> 00:33:04,962
[SPEAKER_01]: But really, when you can properly say, I know this or I understand this,

583
00:33:06,535 --> 00:33:13,641
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about stopping doing anything in relation to it and allowing...

584
00:33:13,661 --> 00:33:14,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Relative stopping, though.

585
00:33:15,402 --> 00:33:16,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Like stopping continues.

586
00:33:17,143 --> 00:33:20,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, doing continues, but it ceases to be a doing.

587
00:33:20,646 --> 00:33:21,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

588
00:33:21,367 --> 00:33:25,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing in terms of movement, attitude towards it, that's all within.

589
00:33:25,350 --> 00:33:25,750
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

590
00:33:25,770 --> 00:33:29,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, whether you choose to focus on it or forget about it, all of that

591
00:33:30,167 --> 00:33:31,669
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes no difference to it.

592
00:33:31,909 --> 00:33:32,650
[SPEAKER_00]: It's within it.

593
00:33:33,290 --> 00:33:36,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning that doesn't stop the doing.

594
00:33:36,193 --> 00:33:37,935
[SPEAKER_00]: It removes the passion from the doing.

595
00:33:37,955 --> 00:33:39,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It removes the wrong order.

596
00:33:39,317 --> 00:33:41,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't stop the doing in relation to it.

597
00:33:41,539 --> 00:33:41,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

598
00:33:42,099 --> 00:33:43,301
[SPEAKER_00]: It stops the doings.

599
00:33:43,621 --> 00:33:47,245
[SPEAKER_00]: They were dependent on the passion, on the wrong order, and everything else.

600
00:33:47,425 --> 00:33:49,047
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't stop all doing.

601
00:33:52,081 --> 00:33:52,762
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what I mean.

602
00:33:53,302 --> 00:33:55,123
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is maintain that right order.

603
00:33:55,164 --> 00:34:03,350
[SPEAKER_00]: This container of the currently felt feeling in you is regardless of whether I weigh my arms, talk to you, talk fast, talk slow, sit down, go silent.

604
00:34:04,811 --> 00:34:05,571
[SPEAKER_00]: I still feel the same.

605
00:34:06,012 --> 00:34:06,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Forget about it.

606
00:34:06,632 --> 00:34:07,353
[SPEAKER_00]: I forget about it.

607
00:34:07,413 --> 00:34:08,653
[SPEAKER_00]: We start talking about something else.

608
00:34:09,714 --> 00:34:11,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I remembered it.

609
00:34:11,876 --> 00:34:13,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Time has passed.

610
00:34:13,337 --> 00:34:16,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Passing of the time is within the duration of the feeling.

611
00:34:16,920 --> 00:34:18,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is not in time.

612
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:21,621
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, oh, it's the same feeling.

613
00:34:21,941 --> 00:34:23,142
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't even have to think about it.

614
00:34:23,962 --> 00:34:26,644
[SPEAKER_00]: I just need to know it, whenever I remember it.

615
00:34:27,224 --> 00:34:30,566
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at the end of the day, I remember, oh, it's still the same feeling.

616
00:34:30,906 --> 00:34:36,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you were basically recollected throughout the day without making effort to be recollected, without thinking it.

617
00:34:36,829 --> 00:34:47,235
[SPEAKER_00]: By... whenever you remember it, so to speak, you remember the correct order, which means, eh... nothing that's happening right now, eh... cannot prove that order, cannot change that order.

618
00:34:47,415 --> 00:34:48,896
[SPEAKER_00]: It's seen against the right light.

619
00:34:50,583 --> 00:34:53,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

620
00:34:55,486 --> 00:34:59,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and there again, I said many times, that's what Buddha said, you can't have too much of mindfulness.

621
00:34:59,748 --> 00:35:04,170
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have too much of the knowledge of what's already there.

622
00:35:05,471 --> 00:35:17,117
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have too much of investigation, you can have too little of, I just can't think about anything, but you can't have too much of knowing that the right principle of mindfulness, the body is already there, the feeling is already there.

623
00:35:18,767 --> 00:35:19,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the body is already there.

624
00:35:19,647 --> 00:35:21,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but not theoretically.

625
00:35:21,268 --> 00:35:22,328
[SPEAKER_00]: The body is already there.

626
00:35:22,808 --> 00:35:23,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the feeling is already there.

627
00:35:23,929 --> 00:35:24,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

628
00:35:24,209 --> 00:35:24,849
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you feel?

629
00:35:25,469 --> 00:35:26,189
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel pleasant.

630
00:35:26,689 --> 00:35:27,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already there.

631
00:35:27,870 --> 00:35:33,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within that pleasure now, all the intentions, everything you choose to do, will be within that.

632
00:35:34,232 --> 00:35:39,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means that type of feeling that's currently enduring for you, will be influencing everything within it.

633
00:35:39,894 --> 00:35:40,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

634
00:35:41,614 --> 00:35:42,414
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not up to you.

635
00:35:42,434 --> 00:35:43,755
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of precedes your choice.

636
00:35:44,375 --> 00:35:45,715
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the basis for your choice.

637
00:35:47,756 --> 00:35:47,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

638
00:35:49,747 --> 00:35:51,728
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also how you stop being attached to pleasure.

639
00:35:54,530 --> 00:35:57,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, oh, it's... it's not up to me.

640
00:35:59,154 --> 00:36:00,054
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subjected to it.

641
00:36:01,796 --> 00:36:06,219
[SPEAKER_00]: So, pursuing it... Can you pursue the pleasure unless it's for you?

642
00:36:07,219 --> 00:36:07,400
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

643
00:36:07,520 --> 00:36:09,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless it's for me to be felt.

644
00:36:09,481 --> 00:36:12,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you assume I'm first and that's the feeling that's my end goal.

645
00:36:12,964 --> 00:36:13,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

646
00:36:14,044 --> 00:36:15,485
[SPEAKER_00]: But wait, I already feel...

647
00:36:16,046 --> 00:36:17,927
[SPEAKER_00]: I already feel the end goal as well.

648
00:36:18,848 --> 00:36:18,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh.

649
00:36:19,983 --> 00:36:29,325
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't have it the way I want it, or similar, which means... Oh, so actually, my pursuit of pleasure is rooted in already feeling the pain of not having it.

650
00:36:34,406 --> 00:36:37,286
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can I change the order of that pain by getting... No.

651
00:36:37,366 --> 00:36:37,946
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

652
00:36:38,226 --> 00:36:39,346
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

653
00:36:39,626 --> 00:36:40,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It's second to this.

654
00:36:40,527 --> 00:36:40,767
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just...

655
00:36:41,027 --> 00:36:42,227
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always second to that.

656
00:36:43,067 --> 00:36:45,828
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be able to pursue pleasure if you don't feel anything.

657
00:36:46,548 --> 00:36:47,788
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's inconceivable.

658
00:36:49,380 --> 00:36:50,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when do you perceive pleasure?

659
00:36:52,041 --> 00:36:57,123
[SPEAKER_00]: When you want to escape the pain and when you don't have enough of pleasure, which is painful.

660
00:36:57,163 --> 00:36:58,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having enough of pleasure is painful.

661
00:36:59,404 --> 00:37:01,364
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you pursue pleasure when you feel pain.

662
00:37:03,045 --> 00:37:08,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are believed that your pursuit and accomplishment of pleasure is somehow gonna kind of...

663
00:37:09,380 --> 00:37:12,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Replace everything and stay with you because you created it.

664
00:37:12,922 --> 00:37:18,644
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only reason you were even making the effort to create that pleasure is because you were subjected to the feeling in the first place.

665
00:37:19,445 --> 00:37:22,626
[SPEAKER_00]: And that pleasure you're chasing is another feeling you're going to be subjected to.

666
00:37:22,846 --> 00:37:22,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

667
00:37:23,366 --> 00:37:25,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you get the pleasure, you can't get the pleasure.

668
00:37:26,188 --> 00:37:26,388
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

669
00:37:26,868 --> 00:37:27,328
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't get it.

670
00:37:27,348 --> 00:37:29,209
[SPEAKER_00]: You can make yourself subjected to pleasure.

671
00:37:29,749 --> 00:37:30,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Circumstantially.

672
00:37:30,270 --> 00:37:31,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Make yourself subjected to pain.

673
00:37:31,850 --> 00:37:35,912
[SPEAKER_00]: You may find that a certain feeling arises that you were hoping for.

674
00:37:36,052 --> 00:37:36,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

675
00:37:36,833 --> 00:37:37,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Or not.

676
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:41,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes you pursue the same thing and, oh, it doesn't feel the same anymore.

677
00:37:41,621 --> 00:37:41,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

678
00:37:42,141 --> 00:37:44,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, oh, wow, I got so much pleasure from this, I didn't expect it.

679
00:37:44,882 --> 00:37:45,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

680
00:37:45,662 --> 00:37:47,222
[SPEAKER_00]: You're always liable to feelings.

681
00:37:48,362 --> 00:37:54,524
[SPEAKER_00]: But for as long as you keep pursuing it in a manner of, I'm first and I'm getting this, you maintain the order that you are first.

682
00:37:54,984 --> 00:37:58,585
[SPEAKER_00]: And you remain completely oblivious to the fact that you are liable to it.

683
00:38:00,238 --> 00:38:01,239
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you suffer.

684
00:38:01,959 --> 00:38:18,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of what you feel, or how you feel it, it's because you just keep trying to... Well, you keep maintaining that discrepancy of the assumption that I'm first and feeling you, like... Because each time you put yourself first, you imply that you're master of things.

685
00:38:18,751 --> 00:38:20,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Master of the body, master of the feeling.

686
00:38:20,653 --> 00:38:27,278
[SPEAKER_00]: And when those things don't go the way you want them, you suffer because... Not because they didn't go the way you want, but because of your mastery caused the...

687
00:38:27,960 --> 00:38:30,821
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no discrepancy that's felt this pain.

688
00:38:31,061 --> 00:38:34,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you wouldn't suffer if somebody comes and cuts down the tree and you sit back.

689
00:38:35,482 --> 00:38:36,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You just wouldn't suffer.

690
00:38:36,443 --> 00:38:36,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

691
00:38:36,883 --> 00:38:39,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the act of cutting the tree is not the suffering.

692
00:38:39,864 --> 00:38:43,505
[SPEAKER_01]: But if someone cuts down the tree while you're trying to keep it up there and holding it there.

693
00:38:43,525 --> 00:38:43,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

694
00:38:43,665 --> 00:38:45,206
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're climbed up at the top.

695
00:38:45,246 --> 00:38:46,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

696
00:38:46,526 --> 00:38:53,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you come down, cutting of the tree still occurs, but no emotional result in you.

697
00:38:54,249 --> 00:38:54,849
[SPEAKER_00]: No suffering.

698
00:38:55,804 --> 00:39:00,207
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you refuse to give up the mastery, and this is my tree, yeah, I don't care.

699
00:39:01,148 --> 00:39:02,929
[SPEAKER_00]: By the time you come down, I've already cut it.

700
00:39:04,731 --> 00:39:05,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have a choice.

701
00:39:05,591 --> 00:39:09,254
[SPEAKER_00]: You can remain like indignant and hold on to what's yours.

702
00:39:10,195 --> 00:39:12,376
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's truly yours, nobody could cut it.

703
00:39:13,357 --> 00:39:14,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody could uproot it.

704
00:39:14,338 --> 00:39:15,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody could destroy it.

705
00:39:16,539 --> 00:39:18,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there really the fact here, the case?

706
00:39:18,741 --> 00:39:18,941
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

707
00:39:19,141 --> 00:39:20,863
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just your own intoxication.

708
00:39:21,783 --> 00:39:22,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a drunk man.

709
00:39:22,884 --> 00:39:23,865
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I can do this.

710
00:39:23,925 --> 00:39:24,525
[SPEAKER_00]: I can fight.

711
00:39:25,625 --> 00:39:27,505
[SPEAKER_00]: A hundred people and I can win.

712
00:39:27,825 --> 00:39:29,386
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you slip and fall by yourself.

713
00:39:30,286 --> 00:39:30,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

714
00:39:31,926 --> 00:39:40,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you choose to refuse to give it up, despite all the evidence that you should.

715
00:39:40,448 --> 00:39:41,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, look, they're coming with the axes.

716
00:39:43,568 --> 00:39:45,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Death, old age, sickness around me and so on.

717
00:39:46,369 --> 00:39:46,969
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

718
00:39:47,429 --> 00:39:48,589
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll keep that all aside.

719
00:39:48,709 --> 00:39:53,170
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all gonna stay for me, because basically you're in charge.

720
00:39:53,190 --> 00:39:53,930
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my practice.

721
00:39:53,970 --> 00:39:54,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

722
00:40:05,042 --> 00:40:11,925
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why even things like aging, death, sickness, are not suffering in themselves.

723
00:40:11,945 --> 00:40:13,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Chopping of the tree doesn't matter.

724
00:40:14,246 --> 00:40:15,567
[SPEAKER_00]: It only matters if you're in it.

725
00:40:19,469 --> 00:40:22,470
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like those arahants in the suttas when the Buddha died.

726
00:40:22,490 --> 00:40:23,630
[SPEAKER_00]: When they said, like, oh...

727
00:40:24,411 --> 00:40:25,611
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, Sariputta died as well.

728
00:40:26,812 --> 00:40:30,053
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, of course, how can that which has arisen not pass?

729
00:40:30,113 --> 00:40:33,395
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's... like, it's inconceivable to grieve, like...

730
00:40:34,236 --> 00:40:34,937
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

731
00:40:35,758 --> 00:40:43,506
[SPEAKER_00]: It requires this extreme level of intoxication to grieve over death.

732
00:40:43,526 --> 00:40:52,836
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a verse somewhere in the Theragata, something like, indifferent towards life, indifferent to life, indifferent to death.

733
00:40:56,922 --> 00:40:58,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you mean the one, I delight not in life?

734
00:40:58,523 --> 00:40:59,084
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

735
00:40:59,284 --> 00:41:00,505
[SPEAKER_00]: I delight not in death.

736
00:41:00,765 --> 00:41:03,027
[SPEAKER_00]: I wait my time, like, hiring his wage.

737
00:41:03,207 --> 00:41:04,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

738
00:41:04,708 --> 00:41:11,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, literally, it's like, you're out, so there's nothing felt, regardless of whether the tree stays up for longer or not.

739
00:41:11,794 --> 00:41:18,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

740
00:41:18,439 --> 00:41:21,362
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to have a preference over weather,

741
00:41:22,697 --> 00:41:24,998
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm subjected to feelings or not.

742
00:41:26,239 --> 00:41:30,320
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the result of you already being subjected to feelings.

743
00:41:32,281 --> 00:41:41,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't stop having a preference, but you can start seeing the right context of that preference, which is that, oh, it's second to the already enduring feeling, which is why I prefer this over that.

744
00:41:42,366 --> 00:41:42,786
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

